# Line endings are part of this repository's provenance guarantee, not a matter of taste.
#
# `viewer/scripts/build-wasm.mjs` fingerprints 28 source files -- everything under
# `crates/worldbuilder-engine/src`, `examples` and `tests`, that crate's `Cargo.toml`, the
# workspace `Cargo.toml` and `Cargo.lock` -- by hashing their **working-tree bytes**, and
# records the digest in
# `viewer/public/wasm/MANIFEST.txt`. With no attributes, those bytes are a property of the
# machine that checked the tree out rather than of the commit:
#
#   * a clone at `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git-for-Windows default) hashes CRLF;
#   * a clone at `core.autocrlf=false`, and every Linux CI runner, hashes LF;
#   * the tree this was authored in held a *mix* -- 14 LF, 9 CRLF and one file with both --
#     and so matched neither.
#
# Three trees, one commit, three different `source-fingerprint` values. `npm run check:wasm`
# reported STALE ARTIFACT on every pristine checkout, and the parity harness -- which
# refuses to report parity against a stale artifact -- refused with it, so the 53,251/0
# parity result could not be reproduced from git by anyone. A gate whose first act on a
# reviewer's machine is a false alarm is a gate that gets switched off.
#
# `viewer/.gitattributes` had already met this exact hazard for the vendored Cesium tree and
# fixed it there. It was not generalised, and the fingerprinted inputs live outside the path
# it covers. This file generalises it: every text file in the repository is stored LF and
# checked out LF on every platform, whatever `core.autocrlf` says, so the fingerprint is
# taken over committed content.
#
# This changes checkouts, not history. Every tracked text blob was already LF in the index
# before this file existed -- `git ls-files --eol` over all 537 tracked files: 291 `i/lf`,
# 210 `i/-text` (binary, 209 of them vendored Cesium assets plus the `.wasm`), 36 `i/none`
# (empty). Zero `i/crlf`, zero `i/mixed`. So `git add --renormalize` stages nothing here;
# what it corrects is the working tree, and the digest that is taken from it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The shipped artifact. Never converted, never diffed as text. `text=auto` would classify it
# as binary anyway; saying so is cheaper than relying on the heuristic for the one file whose
# bytes the whole provenance chain is about.
*.wasm binary

# NOTE: `viewer/.gitattributes` marks `/public/vendor/**` and `/cesium-manifest.txt` as
# `-text`, and attributes in a deeper directory win, so the vendored tree keeps being stored
# and checked out verbatim. That is deliberate and this file must not be read as overriding
# it: the vendored bytes have to stay byte-identical to the npm package for the 395-entry
# sha256 manifest to verify.
